    "taxAmount": "0",
    "balanceDue": "0",
    "notes": "[Houzz #5826912] AP Mental Health\n\nVancouver, Washington, 98663\n\nBy Richard Lord\n\n##[h7kt19xgr5n20260128163102357] Tue, Jan 27, 2026\n\nOverview\n\nWATER INTRUSION FIELD REPORT\n\n  \n\n  \n\nAP Mental Health\n\nInitial Investigation & Drying Log\n\nDates: January 24–27, 2026\n\n  \n\n  \n\n1. Purpose of Visit\n\n  \n\n  \n\nSite visit was initiated to remove a window in the back office area and perform minor cosmetic repairs in preparation for equipment installation. Upon removal of interior finishes, extensive water intrusion and structural deterioration were discovered, prompting immediate mitigation actions.\n\n  \n\n  \n\n2. Discovery of Water Intrusion\n\n  \n\n  \n\nAfter drywall removal around the window opening, the following conditions were observed:\n\n  \n\n  \n\nActive water present inside the wall cavity\n\n  \n\n  \n\nStanding water and ice in stud bays\n\n  \n\n  \n\nSaturated insulation\n\n  \n\n  \n\nElevated moisture in framing members\n\n  \n\n  \n\nDry rot present in multiple locations\n\n  \n\n  \n\nOSB sheathing biologically compromised\n\n  \n\n  \n\nCar-deck style 2×6 roof structure saturated\n\n  \n\n  \n\nDue to active moisture and deteriorated materials, additional openings were created to expose affected assemblies and follow the water intrusion path.\n\n  \n\n  \n\n3. Source & Mechanism of Intrusion (Causation)\n\n  \n\n  \n\nInspection of the exterior flat roof area revealed:\n\n  \n\n  \n\nA pony wall surrounding the roof was capped with segmented finger-joint wood pieces.\n\n  \n\n  \n\nNo waterproofing membrane or flashing was installed beneath the wood cap.\n\n  \n\n  \n\nWood cap joints exhibited movement and cracking, creating direct rainwater entry points.\n\n  \n\n  \n\nBecause the intrusion point was above the weather-resistive barrier, bulk water bypassed the sheathing layer and drained directly into the wall cavity.\n\n  \n\n  \n\nThis represents top-down water intrusion, a failure mode exterior gypsum sheathing cannot mitigate.\n\n  \n\n  \n\n4. Wall Assembly Observations\n\n  \n\n  \n\nExterior walls were constructed using a legacy sheathing assembly consisting of:\n\n  \n\n  \n\nBlack-paper-faced gypsum exterior sheathing\n\n  \n\n  \n\nSiding installed directly over the asphalt-impregnated facer\n\n  \n\n  \n\nThis product served as both sheathing and WRB historically, but it is not designed to resist bulk water from above. Resulting failures included:\n\n  \n\n  \n\ngypsum core disintegration,\n\n  \n\n  \n\nloss of facer integrity, and\n\n  \n\n  \n\ntransfer of moisture into adjacent framing members.\n\n  \n\n  \n\n5. Structural Damage Observed\n\n  \n\n  \n\nDocumented deterioration included:\n\n  \n\n  \n\nExtensive dry rot in pony wall framing\n\n  \n\n  \n\nCompromised top plates of adjoining interior framing\n\n  \n\n  \n\nBiological infestation throughout OSB sheathing layer\n\n  \n\n  \n\nMoisture saturation in car-deck ceiling structure\n\n  \n\n  \n\nSaturated insulation throughout affected cavities\n\n  \n\n  \n\nAt this time, exterior sheathing and compromised framing remain in place so the insurance adjuster can verify conditions and document biological infiltration and dry rot prior to removal.\n\n  \n\n  \n\n6. Access & Exposure Scope\n\n  \n\n  \n\nTo follow the water path and expose affected materials:\n\n  \n\n  \n\n100% of ceiling was removed in the back office space\n\n  \n\n  \n\n20% of ceiling and wall was removed near rear entrance\n\n  \n\n  \n\nAll insulation was removed and disposed of\n\n  \n\n  \n\nExposed assemblies allowed accurate moisture assessment\n\n  \n\n  \n\nOpenings remain controlled to avoid unnecessary exposure of the interior to exterior moisture conditions prior to the adjuster’s visit.\n\n  \n\n  \n\n7. Mitigation & Drying Actions\n\n  \n\n  \n\nFollowing exposure:\n\n  \n\n  \n\nAll affected assemblies were treated with antimicrobial/antifungal agent\n\n  \n\n  \n\nDrying equipment deployed from Jan 25 – Jan 27\n\n  \n\n  \n\nEquipment Deployed\n\n  \n\n  \n\n(1) Dri-Eaz commercial dehumidifier — 18 gallons/day\n\n  \n\n  \n\n(3) Axial air movers — 1,000 CFM each\n\n  \n\n  \n\n(1) HEPA air scrubber — continuous operation for duration\n\n  \n\n  \n\nProjected removal scheduled for Jan 28, pending moisture verification.\n\n  \n\n  \n\n8. Ambient Drying Conditions\n\n  \n\n  \n\nAmbient Humidity: 37%\n\n  \n\n  \n\nTemperature: 62–72°F\n\n  \n\n  \n\nConditions were within acceptable range for structural drying.\n\n  \n\n  \n\n9. Moisture Content Log\n\n  \n\n  \n\nInitial moisture readings taken immediately after exposure were unreliable due to standing water and ice (36–42%\\+). Accurate baseline established after 12 hours of air movement and heat.\n\n  \n\n  \n\nDetailed readings as provided by you are preserved without modification below:\n\n  \n\n  \n\nCeiling – Moisture Content\n\n  \n\n  \n\nAfter 8 hrs: 19.6 | 17.5 | 16.8 | 16.9 | 22.4\n\nDay 2: 17.8 | 14.6 | 13.6 | 15.4 | 18.5\n\nDay 3: 13.8 | 12.6 | 11.3 | 10.4 | 9.1\n\n  \n\n  \n\nFraming – Moisture Content\n\n  \n\n  \n\nAfter 8 hrs: 24 | 18.6 | 30.5 | 32.8 | 18.6\n\nDay 2: 15 | 14.8 | 15.9 | 22.2 | 17.2\n\nDay 3: 21.4 | 11.5 | 12.6 | 17.9 | 12.4\n\n  \n\n  \n\nExterior Sheathing – Moisture Content\n\n  \n\n  \n\nAfter 8 hrs: 16.3 | 27.3 | 33.6 | 23.7 | 42 | 23.7 | 19.9\n\nDay 2: 14.1 | 21.2 | 28.5 | 20.2 | 35.6 | 19.8 | 15.4\n\nDay 3: 13.6 | 14.2 | 16.5 | 18 | 13.7 | 12.2 | 19.2\n\n  \n\n  \n\nRear Door Area\n\n  \n\n  \n\nAfter 8 hrs: 33.6 | 18.9 | 17.4 | 18.2\n\nDay 2: 14.5 | 21.5 | 12.2 | 16.2\n\nDay 3: 10.7 | 14.4 | 9.9 | 15.5\n\n  \n\n  \n\nInside Pony Wall – Stairwell Side\n\n  \n\n  \n\nAfter 8 hrs: 22.7 | 24.8 | 19.6 | 15.9\n\nDay 2: 19.4 | 18.5 | 14.4 | 12.2\n\nDay 3: 14.2 | 13.9 | 10.4 | 11.6\n\n  \n\n  \n\n10. Drying Objective & Status\n\n  \n\n  \n\nIndustry-standard drying objective for Douglas-fir framing members is:\n\n  \n\n  \n\n≤15% Moisture Content prior to enclosure\n\n  \n\n  \n\nAs of Day 3, all monitored assemblies are trending below or near threshold. Based on current rate of decline, all areas are expected to reach target within 24 hours, allowing equipment removal on Jan 28 and transition to reconstruction scope.\n\n  \n\n  \n\n11. Next Steps\n\n  \n\n  \n\nPending adjuster review on-site:\n\n  \n\n  \n\nRemove biologically compromised sheathing\n\n  \n\n  \n\nRemove compromised framing members\n\n  \n\n  \n\nIdentify replacement and reconstruction scope\n\n  \n\n  \n\nRe-establish WRB and cap detail compliant with modern waterproofing standards\n\nWeather\n\nTue, Jan 27\n\nPatchy rain nearby\n\nH 54°\n\nL 32°\n\nPrecipitation: 83%\n\nHumidity: 68%\n\nWind: 6 km/h\n\nLast modified on Jan 27, 2026, 1:14 PM by Richard Lord",
